# Example /etc/pam.d snippet for pam_facial.
#
# Insert as the FIRST auth line, BEFORE the existing password check.
# ALWAYS use "sufficient" — NEVER "requisite" or "required". With
# "sufficient", any non-success result from pam_facial.so (including the
# safe-fallback PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL it returns whenever the camera, the
# model, or enrollment data isn't available) simply falls through to the
# next line instead of aborting the whole stack. Do not remove or comment
# out the existing password auth line below it — that is the fallback
# this module depends on to never lock you out.
#
# Read docs/testing-safely.md BEFORE adding this to any real service
# (sudo, login, a display manager's greeter, etc). Validate first with
# pam_test_harness against the throwaway facial-auth-test service. Never
# add this to sshd first — a mistake there is much harder to recover from
# than a local mistake with a spare root shell open.
#
# facial-auth-control's "System Login" tab can also insert this line for
# you (sudo/gdm-password/sddm/lightdm only, never sshd), gated behind a
# typed confirmation and a 4-of-5 live recognition check performed by the
# privileged helper before it writes anything — see
# src/core/pam/PamServiceConfig.hpp. Editing by hand, as below, works
# identically; use whichever you're more comfortable auditing.

auth    sufficient   pam_facial.so
auth    include      system-auth
